{"timestamp":"2026-05-07T00:30:18.583Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_PGtIPP8lh0m02SsFTANLXxrp","process_id":"61542","turn_id":"019dffd7-953d-7920-bf96-7c19b97e88b7","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/.agents/skills/pr-feedback/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/entire-migrate-lock","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/.agents/skills/pr-feedback/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/pfleidi/.agents/skills/pr-feedback/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pr-feedback\ndescription: >\n  Fetch PR review comments, separate human from bot feedback, and address them differently.\n  Human comments are addressed one at a time with user approval. Bot comments are batched\n  and fixed in one pass. No commits are made — code changes only, user commits on their own terms.\n  Accepts an optional PR number or URL; defaults to the PR for the current branch.\n  Use when the user says \"fix pr comments\", \"address pr feedback\", \"pr feedback\", or \"/pr-feedback\".\n---\n\n# PR Feedback\n\n## 1. Identify the PR\n\nIf the user provided a PR number or URL, use that. Otherwise, detect from the current branch:\n\n```bash\ngh pr view --json number,title,url --jq '.number, .title, .url'\n```\n\nIf no PR exists for the current branch, stop and tell the user.\n\n## 2. Fetch Review Comments\n\nRetrieve all pending review comments:\n\n```bash\ngh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{number}/comments --paginate\n```\n\nAlso fetch top-level review bodies (reviewers sometimes put context there):\n\n```bash\ngh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{number}/reviews --paginate\n```\n\nFilter to unresolved comments only. If there are no unresolved comments, report that to the user and stop — there is nothing to fix.\n\n## 3. Parse, Classify, and Group\n\nFor each comment, extract:\n- **Author** — who left it\n- **Author type** — human or bot (check if the author is a bot account or a known CI/lint tool)\n- **File and line** — where it points\n- **Body** — the actual feedback (verbatim, not paraphrased)\n- **Thread context** — any replies in the same thread (to understand if it was already discussed or resolved conversationally)\n\nGroup comments that refer to the same issue or file region into a single finding.\n\nClassify each finding as **human** or **bot**:\n- **Bot** — author is a GitHub bot, CI system, or automated tool (e.g. `github-actions[bot]`, `codecov[bot]`, linters, static analysis)\n- **Human** — everything else\n\n## 4. Present Findings\n\nPresent two separate sections:\n\n### Human Comments (addressed individually)\n\nNumbered list, ordered by:\n\n1. **Bugs / correctness issues** — reviewer identified broken logic or missing error handling\n2. **Design / architecture feedback** — structural changes, API shape, naming of public interfaces\n3. **Style / nits** — formatting, naming of local variables, minor readability\n\nFor each finding, show:\n- **#N** — `file.go:42` — One-line summary of what the reviewer is asking for\n- **Reviewer** — who asked\n- **Quote** — the key sentence from their comment (verbatim)\n\n### Bot Comments (batched)\n\nNumbered list (continuing the numbering from above), grouped by tool/bot. For each:\n- **#N** — `file.go:42` — One-line summary of the required fix\n- **Bot** — which tool flagged it\n\nEnd with a summary: total human comments, total bot comments, overall assessment of effort.\n\n**STOP and wait for user confirmation.** The user may reorder, skip, or drop items from either section.\n\n## 5. Fix Bot Comments (batched)\n\nFix all confirmed bot comments first — these are mechanical and clearing them reduces noise before the slower human-comment phase.\n\n\n1. For each bot finding:\n   - Read the relevant code\n   - Implement the fix — ONLY the changes needed for that single finding\n   - If a fix is ambiguous or would conflict with a human-comment fix already applied, STOP and ask\n2. After all bot fixes are applied, present a summary table. Do NOT show a diff — the Edit tool already showed each change inline.\n\n| # | Finding | File | Bot | Status |\n|---|---------|------|-----|--------|\n| 8 | Description | path:line | linter-name | Fixed |\n| 9 | Description | path:line | linter-name | Fixed |\n| 11 | Description | path:line | linter-name | Skipped — conflicts with #3 |\n\n3. **STOP and wait.**\n\n## 6. Fix Human Comments (one at a time)\n\nAfter bot fixes are reviewed, work through the confirmed human comments one at a time:\n\n1. State which finding you are addressing (number and one-line description)\n2. Read the relevant code and the full comment thread to understand intent\n3. Implement the fix — ONLY the changes needed for that single finding\n4. **STOP and wait for user approval** before proceeding to the next finding\n\n### Scope Rules\n\n- Make the MINIMAL change that addresses the reviewer's feedback\n- Keep the diff limited to files and lines directly required by the feedback\n- If the feedback requires a behavior-changing code fix, add or update the directly related test in the same fix. Prefer writing the failing test first; otherwise add the test immediately after the code change before moving on.\n- Do NOT rename variables, reformat code, or touch lines outside the feedback scope\n- Do NOT refactor adjacent code, even if it looks related\n- If the reviewer's comment is ambiguous, STOP and ask the user for clarification rather than guessing\n- Do NOT create any git commits — code changes only\n\n## 7. Verify Fixes\n\nAfter all fixes are applied, run the project's lint and test commands scoped to only the changed files and their directly related tests:\n\n- **Lint / static analysis** — run the project's documented lint task, scoped to the files that were modified when the task supports scoping. Prefer lint-specific task wrappers such as `make lint` or `mise run lint` over invoking linter binaries directly. Do not use aggregate `check`, `ci`, or `verify` tasks unless you have confirmed they only run lint/static analysis. If the documented lint task cannot be scoped, run the smallest relevant project lint task.\n- **Tests** — run only the test files that cover the modified code (same package, same module, co-located test files). Do NOT run the full test suite.\n\nIf no project lint task exists, state that explicitly instead of assuming an unavailable linter binary.\n\nShow the COMPLETE output. Do not summarize or truncate.\n\nIf lint or tests fail due to issues introduced by the fixes:\n\n1. Read the error output and identify every failure\n2. Fix all issues — apply the minimal changes needed\n3. Re-run the failing commands\n4. Show the complete output again\n\nCap at **2 fix attempts**. If still failing after 2 rounds, present the remaining failures to the user with full output.\n\nOnce verification passes, show a summary: how many comments were addressed vs skipped, and remind the user to push and re-request review if ready. Do NOT show a diff — the Edit tool already showed each change inline.\n\n## 8. Post-Fix\n\n- If the user asks for adjustments, apply them\n- If the user asks to revert specific fixes, revert ONLY those changes\n- Do NOT commit. The user handles commits on their own terms.\n\n## 9. Reply to PR Comments\n\nAfter the user has committed and pushed, offer to reply to the addressed review threads. **STOP and wait for user approval** before posting any replies.\n\nIf approved, for each addressed finding:\n\n- Reply in the original comment thread using `gh api` with a brief description of what was changed (e.g. \"Fixed — added nil check before dereferencing\" or \"Updated to use the existing `ParseConfig` helper\")\n- Keep replies to one sentence. Do NOT over-explain or repeat the diff.\n- Do NOT reply to skipped or dropped findings\n- After replying, resolve the thread\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2750},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
